    title: "Glaciers and Ice Sheets",
    text: "Glaciers are massive, slow-moving bodies of ice that form in high-altitude mountains or polar regions where temperatures remain freezing year-round. They begin to form when more snow falls in the winter than melts during the brief summer months. Over hundreds or even thousands of years, accumulating layers of heavy snow pile on top of each other. The weight of the upper layers compresses the underlying snow, squeezing out air bubbles and turning it into highly dense glacial ice. Due to their immense weight and the force of gravity, glaciers do not remain stationary. Instead, they slowly flow downhill and across landscapes, behaving like extremely slow-moving rivers of ice. As they slide, glaciers carve out dramatic U-shaped valleys and scrape away rocks, reshaping the terrain. Today, glaciers and ice sheets store about 69 percent of the world's freshwater. However, due to global warming, glaciers worldwide are melting at unprecedented rates, contributing to rising sea levels.",
