    title: "Sound Waves and Hearing",
    text: "Sound is a form of energy that travels in waves, enabling us to communicate, enjoy music, and stay aware of our environment. Sound is created when an object vibrates, such as a guitar string or human vocal cords. This mechanical vibration pushes against nearby air molecules, creating a chain reaction of pressure waves known as longitudinal sound waves. Sound waves require a physical medium—such as air, water, or solid wood—to travel, meaning sound cannot exist in the vacuum of space. When these traveling waves enter our ear canal, they strike a thin membrane called the eardrum, causing it to vibrate. These vibrations are then amplified by three tiny bones in the middle ear before reaching the cochlea, a fluid-filled structure in the inner ear. Inside the cochlea, microscopic hair cells convert the physical vibrations into electrical signals. Finally, these signals travel through the auditory nerve to the brain, which interprets them as recognizable sounds.",
