    title: "Ocean Currents",
    text: "Ocean currents are continuous, predictable, and directed movements of seawater that flow through the world's oceans. These vast marine rivers are generated by a complex interplay of physical forces, including global wind patterns, differences in water temperature and salinity (which affect density), and the rotation of the Earth, a force known as the Coriolis effect. Ocean currents are divided into two main types: surface currents, which are driven primarily by wind, and deep-ocean currents, which are driven by density differences in a process called thermohaline circulation. Together, these currents act as a giant global conveyor belt. They play a critical role in regulating the Earth's climate by transporting warm water and heat from the equator toward the freezing polar regions, and sending cold water back toward the tropics. Without this continuous heat distribution system, equatorial regions would be extremely hot, while polar regions would be far colder, making much of the planet uninhabitable.",
